# Design Selotemna Web

Create a modern corporate real-estate experience that makes Selotemna’s breadth easy to understand and moves visitors toward an inspection or direct conversation. Treat this skill as the governing source for public-page design decisions.

## Load the right references

Read references directly from this file; do not search for replacements before using the bundled source of truth.

1. Read [project-context.md](references/project-context.md) and [design-system.md](references/design-system.md) for every Selotemna design, frontend, content, or review task.
2. Read [homepage-content.md](references/homepage-content.md) and [homepage-section-specification.md](references/homepage-section-specification.md) for any homepage task.
3. Read [real-estate-ux.md](references/real-estate-ux.md) for property discovery, property cards, detail pages, shortlets, inspection booking, enquiries, or mobile contact behaviour.
4. Read [content-rules.md](references/content-rules.md) whenever creating, editing, reviewing, or rendering website copy or business claims.
5. Use [selotemna-logo-reference.png](assets/selotemna-logo-reference.png) only as a visual brand reference. It contains substantial white canvas and is not a production-ready transparent logo.

## Follow this workflow

### 1. Inspect before changing

- Inspect the repository structure, local guidance, `composer.json`, `package.json`, routes, layouts, design tokens, components, fonts, assets, and existing tests.
- Determine whether the application uses Blade, Livewire, Inertia, React, Vue, or another frontend approach.
- Preserve working backend behaviour, authentication, routes, forms, and data bindings unless the task explicitly changes them.
- Reuse established components when they meet the design system; refine them instead of creating duplicates.
- Identify unrelated user changes and leave them untouched.

### 2. State the design read

Before implementation, write one short internal design read covering:

- page type;
- main audience;
- primary user goal;
- dominant visual idea;
- most important responsive risk.

For the homepage, default to:

> A modern corporate real-estate homepage for buyers, investors, diaspora customers, families and businesses, led by confident property imagery, clear service pathways and inspection-first conversion.

Do not ask the user to reconfirm information already present in the bundled references or repository.

### 3. Plan the page as a journey

- Give each section one job, one dominant message, and one primary action.
- Establish who Selotemna is, what it offers, what is available, why the visitor should continue, and how to contact the team.
- Keep Book an Inspection visually primary.
- Keep Explore Properties, WhatsApp, and Call an Agent secondary.
- Use property imagery and typographic hierarchy before adding decorative containers.
- Make the interface usable when listings, testimonials, statistics, or contact fields are missing.

### 4. Apply the brand system exactly

- Use only the approved purple, black, white, and neutral tints in [design-system.md](references/design-system.md).
- Use Sora for headings and Manrope for body and interface text unless an approved project asset overrides them.
- Use Lucide or the existing single outline icon family consistently.
- Preserve the supplied logo proportions and colours; never redraw, recolour, stretch, crop into the mark, or recreate its lettering.
- Prefer white and off-white surfaces, black typography, and restrained purple emphasis.
- Keep modern corporate restraint. Do not turn routine UI into a luxury editorial experiment.

### 5. Protect content truth

- Render only verified property, project, price, location, contact, statistic, testimonial, partner, payment-plan, availability, or documentation information.
- Use approved empty states when data is absent.
- Hide unsupported sections instead of filling them with plausible-looking content.
- Preserve square-bracket placeholders in planning documents; never render them to public production UI.
- Avoid guarantees, unverifiable superlatives, investment-return claims, and legal assurances.
- Follow [content-rules.md](references/content-rules.md) for all copy decisions.

### 6. Implement responsively

- Build mobile-first and verify from 320px upward.
- Keep essential actions available without relying on hover.
- Maintain at least 44px tap targets.
- Use responsive image dimensions and deliberate crops.
- Avoid fixed heights that clip text or controls.
- Reserve mobile safe-area space for any fixed contact bar.
- Prevent fixed UI, drawers, cookie notices, and forms from overlapping one another.

### 7. Verify the result

Before completion:

- Run the project’s relevant formatting, build, and test commands.
- Inspect desktop, tablet, and mobile views in a real browser when browser tooling is available.
- Test navigation, drawers, accordions, forms, loading, validation, success, empty, and missing-data states.
- Check keyboard navigation, focus visibility, headings, labels, alternative text, contrast, and reduced motion.
- Check for horizontal overflow, layout shift, broken routes, missing assets, or placeholder content.
- Compare the result with the page specification and design tokens rather than judging it by vague visual impression.
- Report unresolved content and technical blockers precisely.

## Maintain visual restraint

Reject these patterns unless an existing approved page clearly requires them:

- purple gradients used as the main visual idea;
- glassmorphism or backdrop blur on scrolling content;
- a generic centred hero followed by three equal feature cards;
- card treatment on every section;
- excessive pills, floating badges, stat strips, or decorative icons;
- search controls in the homepage hero before the listing inventory justifies them;
- auto-playing hero carousels;
- large blocks of text placed over busy property images;
- fake testimonials, customer portraits, partner logos, or performance figures;
- foreign stock properties presented as Selotemna projects;
- motion that delays access to information;
- a WhatsApp-green accent that breaks the approved palette;
- a redesign of admin or internal product UI under this public-site skill.

## Handle task boundaries

- For a request limited to planning or review, do not modify code.
- For a request to implement, complete the requested change and verify it.
- For missing property records or contact details, continue with safe conditional rendering and report the blocker; do not fabricate data.
- For a request that conflicts with the design system, explain the conflict and follow the user’s explicit decision.
- For a new public page not covered by the homepage specification, reuse the design-system rules and real-estate UX patterns while tailoring the page narrative to its goal.
